SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system operates in a multi-user environment and manages user access to resources based on roles. It supports role hierarchy, temporary role delegation, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. The system ensures authorization correctness, consistency in delegation and permissions, liveness in request processing, and safety under concurrency. Users are assigned security clearances of High, Medium, or Low, which dictate their access levels and ability to delegate roles or approve requests. The system processes requests in FIFO order but allows for exceptions under certain conditions, such as emergency situations.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users can only submit requests that match or are below their security clearance level.
- Delegated roles must be revoked after a fixed time or by an administrator.
- Emergency mode allows bypassing FIFO order for high-classification requests.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles can be temporarily ignored during Emergency mode.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Triggering Emergency Mode
In Normal mode, exactly two administrators are required to trigger the Emergency mode.

R2: Duration of Emergency Mode
Emergency mode is temporary, remaining active for a fixed duration, with an option for two administrators to manually end it early.

R3: Behavior During Emergency Mode
- R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests.
- R3.2: Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles.
- R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges.

R4: Post-Emergency Mode Operations
- R4.1: Revoke emergency privileges.
- R4.2: Revoke roles delegated during the emergency.
- R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials.
```

